WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.360
All right, let's just jump right into it.

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:06.760
Today, we are looking at something that honestly turns a lot of what we think about game design

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:08.440
completely on its head.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:12.480
It's a new approach, backed by some serious science, and we're not talking about shinier

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.360
graphics or bigger maps here.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.320
We're talking about how to tap into the human brain to build something that lasts way longer

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.760
than just a game.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:24.960
But let's start with a question that I guarantee every single person listening has asked themselves.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:26.280
You know the feeling, right?

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:29.880
You find a game, you're absolutely obsessed, you're sinking hours and hours into it,

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:31.960
and then just like that, you stop.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:33.920
You just don't play anymore.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:34.920
Why does it happen?

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:38.160
Why do we just walk away from games we thought we loved?

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.800
Well the answer is probably not what you think.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.400
See, according to the research we're diving into, it's not that we get bored, it's

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:50.100
because the game itself, the very machine built to keep us hooked, has been training

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.240
our brain to be disappointed.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.920
That is a wild idea, so let's really break that down.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:01.880
So this gets us to what you could call the player burnout problem, and it's a huge paradox.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:05.520
Because it turns out the systems that are designed to keep us playing are often the

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.920
very reason we end up quitting for good.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:12.200
I mean, think about it, how do most games keep you coming back?

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:13.440
It's all about loot, right?

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.200
An endless stream of new gear, better items, bigger rewards.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:20.720
But what this really does is put you on this psychological treadmill.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:24.680
The game has to keep dangling a bigger, shinier carrot, and you have to run faster and

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:28.120
faster just to get that same buzz you felt at the beginning.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:30.040
And that is just not sustainable.

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:33.880
And this is where we get to peek under the hood at the neuroscience of it all.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:37.560
This entire system runs on a brain chemical you've definitely heard about,

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:38.480
dopamine.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:42.440
But the way games use it creates this thing called the dopamine treadmill.

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:47.040
Okay, so this is the key concept right here, reward prediction error.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.560
Your brain doesn't just get a buzz from getting a reward,

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:53.080
it gets a much bigger kick from the surprise of that reward.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:56.520
See, dopamine fires like crazy when the thing you get is better than what you

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:57.560
expected.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:00.360
To your brain, predictability is just boring.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:04.440
So when you first start playing, the expectation of getting that cool

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:09.280
legendary item and the reality of actually getting it are both super exciting.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:12.360
But over time, your brain just gets used to it, it adapts, and

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.840
that dopamine hit gets weaker and weaker and weaker.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.480
The magic just starts to fade, and this kicks off a really nasty cycle.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:22.720
You get a reward, you feel good for a second, but

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:26.480
then your brain adapts, it's a process called hedonic adaptation.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:30.800
Now to get that same good feeling, you need an even bigger, even better reward.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.560
The game is suddenly locked in this impossible arms race against your own brain

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:36.000
chemistry.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.560
And this isn't just a theory, you can actually measure it.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.680
Research shows that after just 45 to 60 minutes of playing,

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:44.520
your brain's dopamine response actually starts to shrink.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.440
So even if the rewards stay the same,

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:49.360
the hit that feeling you get keeps getting smaller.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:51.720
The game literally physically becomes less fun,

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:54.040
the longer you play in a single session.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:57.960
Okay, so if that reward loop is basically a dead end, what's the solution?

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:02.080
Well, the answer is in a different, and frankly, way more powerful part of our

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:04.160
brain, the social brain.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:09.760
This is what can create what we're going to call a sustainable social loop.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:13.640
And when you put them side by side, the difference is just night and day.

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:15.680
Reward loops are all dopamine.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:19.240
They burn out super fast, and eventually the game starts to feel like a job.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:20.480
But social loops?

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:24.160
They combine dopamine with another amazing chemical, oxytocin.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:26.520
You've probably heard of it as the bonding hormone.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:29.320
This combo creates a system that's not just sustainable,

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:32.280
it actually builds a real genuine community.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:35.520
And get this, it's absolutely fascinating.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.880
FMRI studies has actually watched people's brains, and

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:42.440
they've shown that the reward centers in your brain can light up more when you

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:47.200
share a win with your friends than when you accomplish something all by yourself.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:52.520
Our brains are hardwired to feel better about shared success than solo glory.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:54.320
And here it is.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:56.640
This is the knockout punch.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:59.040
Even when a player is completely burnt out on loot,

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:02.840
when their dopamine system is so tapped out that literally nothing feels fun

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:05.680
anymore, that social pull of oxytocin,

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.880
that connection to their friends can still bring them back.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:12.240
It is the single most powerful retention tool there is.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:13.360
Period.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:15.040
So the science is crystal clear.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:17.000
Social connection is everything.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.000
But okay, how do you actually build a game around that?

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.360
Well, this brings us to a whole new blueprint for

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:26.600
one of the most basic parts of any online game, matchmaking.

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:29.720
The core idea here is really counterintuitive.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:33.680
It says you should forget about trying to find the perfectly balanced match

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:35.200
every single time.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:39.240
The best strategy is to build fast and slow.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:43.920
What that means is, at first, get players into a game as quickly as possible.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.560
But over time, the system should start to care less about speed and skill,

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:50.600
and care more about social quality,

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:54.040
about matching you with people you actually vibe with.

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:56.680
And the data on this is just huge.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:57.560
Look at this.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:01.440
Raw data from a major gaming platform showed that on the very first day,

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:04.680
the overall player retention was about 53%.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:07.640
But if you look at players who made just one friend in the game,

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:12.000
that number jumps to a massive 68%.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:13.600
Let's just sit with that for a second.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:16.040
That's a 28% relative lift,

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:21.200
a 28% higher chance that someone will come back and play your game the next day,

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:23.040
just because they made a friend.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:24.320
That's not a minor adjustment.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:26.480
That is an absolute game changer,

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:29.400
and it's driven entirely by social connection.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:32.160
So what does this new kind of matchmaker actually do?

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:35.080
Well, first, it should try to group the same people together

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:37.480
over and over again to build familiarity.

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:40.840
Second, it should actively create shared struggle.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:44.240
Look, real friendships aren't made in easy, forgettable wins.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:45.680
They're forged in fire

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:48.840
when you have to team up to overcome a real challenge.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:50.600
And that leads to the final point.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:54.880
The best matchmaking system actually isn't always fair.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:58.680
Now, these insights are obviously massive for designing better games,

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:02.840
but the implications here, they go way, way beyond just gaming.

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:08.400
You know, the famous Rat Park experiment showed this exact thing decades ago.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.280
They found that rats who were isolated

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:13.360
would just compulsively take drugs until it killed them.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:15.760
But when they put rats in a rich social park

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:17.840
with other rats and things to do,

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:19.800
they pretty much ignored the drugs.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:21.760
It proves a profound point.

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:26.040
The opposite of addiction isn't sobriety, it's connection.

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:27.480
So the big takeaway here

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:30.680
for anyone building any kind of online community is this.

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:33.280
All those tiny daily social interactions,

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:36.280
the little high fives and words of encouragement from friends,

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:40.680
those micro affirmations are a much more powerful and sustainable tool

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:43.240
than massive, expensive content updates.

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:47.880
Your community is the content, and it's content that never runs out.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:50.880
And when you really understand these social mechanics,

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:53.720
when you have the blueprint for building deep connections,

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:57.600
it leads to one final and honestly pretty provocative idea.

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:00.960
If we can map out what makes a strong social bond,

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:03.520
what happens if we feed that map to an AI?

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:05.920
The source we're looking at makes a truly bold claim

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:08.560
that an algorithm could potentially build you a social circle

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:11.480
that makes you happier than one you chose yourself.

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:15.560
Which leaves us with a final, fascinating question to chew on.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:18.600
I mean, we let algorithms pick our movies, our music,

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:20.560
even our romantic partners.

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:24.640
What happens when we turn that same powerful logic toward our friendships?

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:28.720
If an AI could analyze your personality, your play style, your sense of humor,

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:32.400
and build your perfect team of friends, should you let it?

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:35.680
It's a pretty wild and maybe a little unsettling thought to end on.

